WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.600 --> 00:00:11.040
One of the first steps in annotation is to&nbsp;
identify all of the genes within a contig and

00:00:11.040 --> 00:00:17.040
to determine the number of isoforms of each gene.&nbsp;
Remember that a gene is a DNA sequence that can

00:00:17.040 --> 00:00:23.680
encode at least one functional RNA and protein&nbsp;
product. Each gene in the Drosophila genome has

00:00:23.680 --> 00:00:29.600
a unique gene name. We will use the Genome&nbsp;
Browser to learn more about gene structure.

00:00:29.600 --> 00:00:37.920
Go to the GEP UCSC Genome Browser gateway&nbsp;
page and select the D. melanogaster, July 2014

00:00:37.920 --> 00:00:45.760
gene assembly. Enter "contig1" into the search term&nbsp;
box and click "submit." The default view shows the

00:00:45.760 --> 00:00:53.600
entire length of contig1, which consists of 11 000&nbsp;
nucleotides. The FlyBase Genes track shows the D.

00:00:53.600 --> 00:01:00.480
melanogaster genes on the "pack" display mode. With&nbsp;
this view, we can see the gene and isoform names to

00:01:00.480 --> 00:01:05.520
the left of each feature. Many evidence tracks&nbsp;
have different display modes that you can use

00:01:05.520 --> 00:01:13.520
to control how the UCSC genome browser renders&nbsp;
overlapping features. For example, let's change

00:01:13.520 --> 00:01:19.920
the display mode for the FlyBase Genes track&nbsp;
to "dense" and click "refresh." The overlapping

00:01:19.920 --> 00:01:26.000
features are now rendered in a single line. To&nbsp;
determine how many genes are in contig1, we will

00:01:26.000 --> 00:01:32.480
count the number of features in the FlyBase Genes&nbsp;
track. Each feature consists of one or more boxes

00:01:32.480 --> 00:01:39.360
which correspond to exons. The lines that&nbsp;
connect two adjacent boxes correspond to introns.

00:01:39.360 --> 00:01:45.760
A gene is composed of a collection of boxes,&nbsp;
or exons, and lines that connect them together,

00:01:45.760 --> 00:01:53.280
or introns. The gap between two adjacent genes is&nbsp;
known as the intergenic region. In this example,

00:01:53.280 --> 00:02:00.400
contig1 contains three different genes and&nbsp;
each gene has multiple exons and introns.

00:02:00.400 --> 00:02:07.920
Type contig1:1-5800 into the&nbsp;
search terms box and then press "enter" to zoom

00:02:07.920 --> 00:02:14.000
into the region where the first gene is located&nbsp;
within contig1. Change the display mode for the

00:02:14.000 --> 00:02:20.560
FlyBase Genes track to "full" and then click on the&nbsp;
"refresh" button. Now we can see that there are three

00:02:20.560 --> 00:02:29.600
sets of exons and introns that correspond to the&nbsp;
three isoforms of the gene CG32165. An isoform is

00:02:29.600 --> 00:02:35.440
an alternative form of a gene that results from&nbsp;
alternative splicing or the use of different sets

00:02:35.440 --> 00:02:43.120
of transcribed exons in the processed mRNA. Each&nbsp;
isoform of a gene has a different mRNA sequence.

00:02:43.120 --> 00:02:49.920
However, some isoforms may have the same amino&nbsp;
acid sequences because they use the same set

00:02:49.920 --> 00:02:58.640
of coding exons. Let's look at the CG32165 gene&nbsp;
and examine the three isoforms in more detail.

00:02:58.640 --> 00:03:05.200
The labels at the left side of the FlyBase Genes&nbsp;
track correspond to the names of the mRNA. In

00:03:05.200 --> 00:03:11.600
Drosophila, the part of the name before the last&nbsp;
dash corresponds to the gene name, while the suffix

00:03:11.600 --> 00:03:23.520
after the dash, such as RC, RB, or RA, corresponds&nbsp;
to the name of the isoform. For example, CG32165-RC

00:03:23.520 --> 00:03:33.040
corresponds to the C isoform of CG32165. The arrows&nbsp;
within each intron denote the orientation of the

00:03:33.040 --> 00:03:41.920
gene relative to the contig. Because the arrows&nbsp;
point from right to left, CG32165 is on the

00:03:41.920 --> 00:03:49.040
complementary strand. This means that the first&nbsp;
transcribed exon is located at the right side

00:03:49.040 --> 00:03:58.560
of the image. Isoform C has nine exons. Isoform&nbsp;
B also has nine exons, but note that exon one is

00:03:58.560 --> 00:04:05.920
shorter. This isoform has a different transcription&nbsp;
start site. Isoform A only has eight exons,

00:04:05.920 --> 00:04:12.800
the result of a mode of alternative splicing known&nbsp;
as intron retention. Let's zoom out to the entire

00:04:12.800 --> 00:04:20.080
contig1 and look at the spd2 gene. Enter contig1 into the "enter position or search terms" text

00:04:20.080 --> 00:04:28.560
box and then press "enter." We can see that there is&nbsp;
only one isoform of this gene, isoform A. Therefore

00:04:28.560 --> 00:04:36.880
only one kind of mRNA is transcribed. Finally, let's&nbsp;
look at the tra gene. As indicated by the RA and RB

00:04:36.880 --> 00:04:45.040
suffixes, there are two isoforms of the tra gene:
isoform A and isoform B. Each isoform has three

00:04:45.040 --> 00:04:53.600
exons, but note that exon 2 is longer in isoform&nbsp;
B than in isoform A. This is an example of another

00:04:53.600 --> 00:05:03.680
mode of alternative splicing that is caused by&nbsp;
the use of an alternative splice acceptor site.

